00:01:47.327 --> 00:01:51.689
How to think, create and
lead in unconventional ways.

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:53.086
Welcome, Sarah.

00:01:53.086 --> 00:01:54.017
Thanks for joining me.

00:01:54.017 --> 00:01:57.497
I am so excited for
our conversation today.

00:01:57.497 --> 00:02:01.049
&gt;&gt; Matt thank you so much I'm excited to
be here and if you could see my face right

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:04.893
now you'd have a huge smile on it because
I'm very excited for our conversation.

00:02:04.893 --> 00:02:07.534
&gt;&gt; Well I've got a big smile too,
so let's get started.

00:02:07.534 --> 00:02:10.882
The D school is all about
helping students and

00:02:10.882 --> 00:02:14.156
executives learn design thinking skills
&gt;&gt; For

00:02:14.156 --> 00:02:18.082
those who aren't familiar with design
thinking, can you define what it is?

00:02:18.082 --> 00:02:22.381
And how it can help us personally
as well as professionally?

00:02:22.381 --> 00:02:24.286
&gt;&gt; I'm happy to start there.

00:02:24.286 --> 00:02:28.766
So many people have this idea of design,
that is really about the kind

00:02:28.766 --> 00:02:33.556
of visual aspects of the world around
us like the aesthetic qualities, or

00:02:33.556 --> 00:02:38.672
maybe the way that your room feels after
you rearrange the furniture, right?

00:02:38.672 --> 00:02:43.702
And those are definitely aspects of
design and design has through its long

00:02:43.702 --> 00:02:48.832
history really been kind of poking its
nose into lots of different places.

00:02:48.832 --> 00:02:53.649
And what I mean by that is, those same
ways that you can think about being

00:02:53.649 --> 00:02:58.708
intentional about what your room feels
like or how your phone is designed,

00:02:58.708 --> 00:03:02.824
the way in which you know a glass
fits in your hand perfectly.

00:03:02.824 --> 00:03:07.089
Those same ideas can
actually be extrapolated and

00:03:07.089 --> 00:03:12.174
used to design experiences and
services and even systems.

00:03:12.174 --> 00:03:16.217
And so we're in an era now where
design is being used not just for

00:03:16.217 --> 00:03:21.505
all of those aesthetic purposes, but also
to think about what should be created.

00:03:21.505 --> 00:03:24.510
How do we actually deliver
services that are better for

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.459
the people who are accessing them or
less expensive?

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:30.710
Thinking about how do we actually
like redesign a complex system?.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:35.029
For example, one of our fellows a few
years ago was working on redesigning

00:03:35.029 --> 00:03:39.695
the way in which substitute teaching
happens in the United States, like really

00:03:39.695 --> 00:03:43.961
interesting, complex multivariable
kinds of projects and problems.

00:03:43.961 --> 00:03:48.889
And so design thinking is kind of
the vocabulary that sits underneath that,

00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:49.434
right?

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:53.546
It's a way that people who may
have a formal design training or

00:03:53.546 --> 00:03:58.367
may come from lots of different
disciplines can start to use the same sets

00:03:58.367 --> 00:04:00.502
of practices both the skills and

00:04:00.502 --> 00:04:05.666
the mindsets of design to improve those
services, improve those products.

00:04:05.666 --> 00:04:10.257
And even think really creatively about
like what the future of your business

00:04:10.257 --> 00:04:13.998
could look like or how you might
tackle a large social system.

00:04:13.998 --> 00:04:19.084
&gt;&gt; Wow, it sounds like the design
thinking principles can be applied to so

00:04:19.084 --> 00:04:21.987
many different processes and systems.

00:04:21.987 --> 00:04:26.694
And in what you just described it really
underlies that there's a science to it.

00:04:26.694 --> 00:04:30.950
It's creative and it's artful but
there's also a science to it and

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:34.908
I personally have been exposed to it and
find it fascinating for

00:04:34.908 --> 00:04:37.610
how we can design experiences for people.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.714
So thank you for sharing that.

00:04:39.714 --> 00:04:44.977
We recently spoke with GSB professor
Chip Heath about the curse of knowledge,

00:04:44.977 --> 00:04:49.121
knowing too much about what we do and
share and how it can lead to

00:04:49.121 --> 00:04:55.041
miscommunication and a lack of connection
with those that we're communicating with.

00:04:55.041 --> 00:05:00.475
Your focus in your book on widening our
lens strikes me as a way to combat that

00:05:00.475 --> 00:05:05.669
course of knowledge and be more
creative and relevant at the same time.

00:05:05.669 --> 00:05:10.748
Can you tell us a bit more about what
you mean by widening our lens and

00:05:10.748 --> 00:05:12.936
how can we actually do that.

00:05:12.936 --> 00:05:15.779
&gt;&gt; Sure, widening your lens.

00:05:15.779 --> 00:05:19.769
I use that framing just because as
somebody who's a photographer, I think

00:05:19.769 --> 00:05:24.013
a lot about how your perspective is really
altered by not just the equipment that

00:05:24.013 --> 00:05:27.877
you're using, but where you're
standing in relation to the subject or

00:05:27.877 --> 00:05:30.189
the scene that you're trying to capture.

00:05:30.189 --> 00:05:34.057
And in design you want to have a strong
point of view about what you're making,

00:05:34.057 --> 00:05:38.045
you want to to understand the needs, you
want to to understand the context and then

00:05:38.045 --> 00:05:41.674
you want to say like well I think that
it's going to work best if and then you

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:45.997
really establish that strong point of view
is one of those foundational principles.

00:05:45.997 --> 00:05:50.551
But one of the things you have to
recognize is that you don't have the view,

00:05:50.551 --> 00:05:52.593
you have a point of view, right?

00:05:52.593 --> 00:05:53.148
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:05:53.148 --> 00:05:57.307
&gt;&gt; And really understanding like well, how
is someone else going to experience this

00:05:57.307 --> 00:05:59.553
product or service that I'm developing?

00:05:59.553 --> 00:06:04.310
Or what if I think about what the effect
of launching this thing might be a few

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.285
generations into the future?

00:06:06.285 --> 00:06:09.630
And so there are all different
ways to stretch your perspective.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.372
Both to be thinking about how
could this be better designed,

00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:13.836
how could it be more effective?

00:06:13.836 --> 00:06:18.420
How could it be more relevant for
more people and even thinking about, what

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:23.441
are some of the potentially unintended
consequences that could happen as I create

00:06:23.441 --> 00:06:28.777
this fantastic popular successful piece of
work, whatever it is that you're doing.

00:06:28.777 --> 00:06:33.565
So widening your lens is one of those
core ideas in human centered design.

00:06:33.565 --> 00:06:37.187
In that very often you are not
designing for yourself.

00:06:37.187 --> 00:06:39.699
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And you kind of have to get out of your

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:43.223
own way to effectively design
with others needs in mind.

00:06:43.223 --> 00:06:45.991
And so
that idea of shifting your perspective,

00:06:45.991 --> 00:06:49.948
widening your aperture your lens,
is just critically important.

00:06:49.948 --> 00:06:51.536
And there are lots of ways to do that.

00:06:51.536 --> 00:06:55.886
And some of the practices in creative
acts are great ways to start their

00:06:55.886 --> 00:07:00.532
practices around how do you have a very
open ended interview interaction,

00:07:00.532 --> 00:07:05.173
how do you try to shadow someone for a day
&gt;&gt; I heard you say two things that I think

00:07:05.173 --> 00:07:09.275
are absolutely critical to getting
out of that curse of knowledge and

00:07:09.275 --> 00:07:12.259
how design thinking and
being creative can help.

00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:16.682
And one is understanding that it is
other focused you talked about human

00:07:16.682 --> 00:07:17.870
centered design.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:21.251
So you're not designing for
yourself you're designing for others.

00:07:21.251 --> 00:07:23.074
When you communicate you're
not communicating for

00:07:23.074 --> 00:07:24.697
yourself you're communicating for others.

00:07:24.697 --> 00:07:28.925
So first taking that perspective and
then the second thing you said which is so

00:07:28.925 --> 00:07:33.088
important is to get out of your own way
that we actually make it more difficult

00:07:33.088 --> 00:07:37.186
for ourselves and it sounds like some
of the activities in your book and that

00:07:37.186 --> 00:07:41.380
are practiced at the D school can really
help people with both parts of that.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:44.176
focusing in the right place and
then getting out of our own way.

00:07:44.176 --> 00:07:45.742
Did I get that right?

00:07:45.742 --> 00:07:47.167
&gt;&gt; Yeah that's exactly right.

00:07:47.167 --> 00:07:52.077
And we all bring our own life experience,
our own biases our own opinions and

00:07:52.077 --> 00:07:57.064
perspectives and so you often need a way
similar to how we talk about deferring

00:07:57.064 --> 00:08:00.910
judgment when you're trying
to generate new ideas.

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:05.250
You actually need to set aside those
assumptions that you have, those biases,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:09.784
those perspectives that you have, to make
sure they are not too influential in your

00:08:09.784 --> 00:08:13.410
interpretation or
understanding of what the needs are.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:16.277
&gt;&gt; Wherever I think about what
we've talked about so far,

00:08:16.277 --> 00:08:20.980
part of the design process really
involves introspection and reflection.

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:24.066
I'm curious if you can articulate
why this is so important.

00:08:24.066 --> 00:08:26.261
And what are some of
the specific tools and

00:08:26.261 --> 00:08:30.290
techniques that you recommend to help
people do this type of self learning?

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:36.408
&gt;&gt; Well, it's interesting that you just
framed up this idea of self learning,

00:08:36.408 --> 00:08:40.763
because I often think about design
as a process of learning in general.

00:08:40.763 --> 00:08:44.491
So when you're starting out to
design something new whether

00:08:44.491 --> 00:08:48.002
it's reimagining the substitute
teaching ecosystem,

00:08:48.002 --> 00:08:52.533
like I mentioned our former fellow
Joe Violet did, or something much more

00:08:52.533 --> 00:08:57.380
concrete and physical, you're embarking
on a journey of learning, right?

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:02.520
You are starting out with less
understanding and information and insight.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.780
&gt;&gt; And fewer ideas about where you're
going to land the thing that you're

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.613
going to create, then you will
ultimately find yourself with.

00:09:10.613 --> 00:09:15.602
And just like any other learning process,
understanding what's going well and

00:09:15.602 --> 00:09:19.320
what's not going well and
what could be improved.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:25.600
That is a vital part of really
having ownership over that process.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:31.169
So we do a lot of work with our students
and other learners around tools for

00:09:31.169 --> 00:09:35.370
reflection, thinking about,
what just happened and

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:41.070
was it as productive as effective as
powerful as we hoped that it was?

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:42.550
What can be improved?

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:44.479
And maybe even then some
bigger picture questions.

00:09:44.479 --> 00:09:48.848
So I'll give you two examples of
particular practices that we use.

00:09:48.848 --> 00:09:53.375
One is an individual practice which
is called what so what now it.

00:09:53.375 --> 00:09:57.284
Letizia burritos company RL and
some of her colleagues created and

00:09:57.284 --> 00:10:01.471
what they found is that when they ask
students to just kind of generically

00:10:01.471 --> 00:10:05.376
reflect in an open ended way on how
class went or what they learned or

00:10:05.376 --> 00:10:10.095
what a particular process let's say
they were teaching prototyping that day.

00:10:10.095 --> 00:10:13.712
Okay, how did that go,
the students come up with some interesting

00:10:13.712 --> 00:10:16.665
things
&gt;&gt; But when they give them a particular

00:10:16.665 --> 00:10:21.388
framework like what so what now what,
which is simply the idea that you separate

00:10:21.388 --> 00:10:25.920
you disaggregate and distinguish
between those three different buckets.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.510
So what just happened?

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:28.880
Name all the details.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:29.950
What did it smell like?

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:31.870
What was the feeling that you had?

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.690
Specifically what happened?

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:37.200
And then, so what, which is like,
well, what were the implications?

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.520
What did that lead to?

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:40.540
What happened as a result?

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:43.110
And now what, which is like,
well, what does that mean?

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:44.830
What does that suggest for the future?

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.620
How do you feel about that?

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:49.018
Those bigger introspective questions.

00:10:49.018 --> 00:10:53.704
With that particular rubric the
reflections that the students do are of

00:10:53.704 --> 00:10:55.266
much higher caliber and

00:10:55.266 --> 00:11:00.362
much more powerful as a learning tool as
a self learning tool as you just said.

00:11:00.362 --> 00:11:04.510
So that's one great example of
a practice on the personal level.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:10.310
And then one of my favorites from this
collection is called I like I wish how to.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:13.517
And that is a practice that we've been
using at the D school from the very

00:11:13.517 --> 00:11:14.650
beginning.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:20.010
And it really is wonderful as a group
to go through experience together.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:23.270
That could be a day of class that
could be a workshop that could be.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:29.210
A tough year at your company and
do a group reflection using those prompts.

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:32.130
I like, I wish and how to.

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.135
And that is a non-judgmental or

00:11:34.135 --> 00:11:39.550
less judgmental way to still be able to
say like, hey, here's what went well.

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:41.170
Here's what I wish for.

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:44.954
And here's some other ideas that are
starting to come up about what we could do

00:11:44.954 --> 00:11:46.770
to change things or improve things.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:51.815
&gt;&gt; Well, I think we have bingo now,
because anybody who knows me and

00:11:51.815 --> 00:11:56.645
listens to this podcast knows that, what
so, what now, what is a mantra that I sing

00:11:56.645 --> 00:12:00.320
the praises for all the time and
I love how you apply it.

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:04.583
In terms of self learning in terms
of feedback and introspection and

00:12:04.583 --> 00:12:09.540
having tools as you've discussed to help
us do that is really really helpful.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:14.264
Because for many of us it's really hard to
take the time to do that reflection and

00:12:14.264 --> 00:12:16.249
having specific tools can help.

00:12:16.249 --> 00:12:20.822
In thinking back to all of the various
activities and techniques in your book,

00:12:20.822 --> 00:12:24.370
can you identify one that has
really helped you personally?

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:29.426
&gt;&gt; Yeah I mentioned I like I wish
which has been quite important for

00:12:29.426 --> 00:12:33.831
me the category that I'll share that
I think has been really useful for

00:12:33.831 --> 00:12:36.310
me particularly in a leadership role.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:39.098
To remember to practice consistently,

00:12:39.098 --> 00:12:44.018
is around using those first few minutes
of any meeting or work session,

00:12:44.018 --> 00:12:48.938
to set the right tone and to get people
already starting to practice some

00:12:48.938 --> 00:12:53.760
of the behaviors that you want to
see come out in the work to be done.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:55.824
&gt;&gt; Through some kind of warm up activity.

00:12:55.824 --> 00:13:00.013
And I have kind of a strong point of
view that like icebreakers are fine, but

00:13:00.013 --> 00:13:04.135
usually what I'm doing is not trying
to just get people to break the ice and

00:13:04.135 --> 00:13:06.190
know each other a little bit.

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.914
I'm actually wanting people to
start to rehearse the same behavior

00:13:09.914 --> 00:13:12.660
that we need to then practice as a group.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.550
So the activities that are around.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:17.000
Building trust with someone.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:20.193
That's a wonderful warm up to do if
you're going to have a group then

00:13:20.193 --> 00:13:22.283
start to launch an interviewing process or

00:13:22.283 --> 00:13:25.490
even if you're just going to try
to make a hard decision, right?

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:28.370
That's a really good time
to use that as a team.

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:33.195
Or the activity that's around coming up
with lots of metaphors and similes for

00:13:33.195 --> 00:13:35.035
complex problems or topics.

00:13:35.035 --> 00:13:39.259
That is a great warm up to use when you
are launching a storytelling session or

00:13:39.259 --> 00:13:43.483
you are trying to figure out how you're
going to present your really cool but

00:13:43.483 --> 00:13:46.058
kind of esoteric new idea
to a group of people.

00:13:46.058 --> 00:13:52.589
So I like to really match the nature
of the warm up with the work at hand.

00:13:52.589 --> 00:13:55.527
And if I'm the one who's leading
the class or the workshop,

00:13:55.527 --> 00:13:57.810
I'll then be very transparent for folks.

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:01.257
Hey, the reason we started with this is
because it's going to lead right into what

00:14:01.257 --> 00:14:03.140
we're doing today as a group.

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:06.942
And I find that that helps people get
like right into the right mindset and

00:14:06.942 --> 00:14:09.450
the right kind of skill set for
the work at hand.

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:15.300
&gt;&gt; I find that so refreshing because so
many people do icebreakers just for

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:20.386
the sake of doing an icebreaker in this
notion that the activity you initiate

00:14:20.386 --> 00:14:25.790
with can actually serve as a signpost for
what's to come or get people to rehearse

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:31.060
and practice skills that you'll be
developing, I think is really powerful.

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:35.303
And I encourage everybody to think about
when you initiate a meeting a presentation

00:14:35.303 --> 00:14:37.190
any kind of collaboration?

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:41.016
How can you start with some kind of
activity that gets people focused and

00:14:41.016 --> 00:14:45.130
engaged but also begins working on
the skills you're trying to build?

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:46.538
I really like that idea and

00:14:46.538 --> 00:14:49.360
will immediately begin
applying it in the work I do.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:54.851
Before we end I'd like to ask you the same
three questions I asked everyone who joins

00:14:54.851 --> 00:14:56.900
me, are you up for that?

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:57.440
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:58.210
&gt;&gt; Excellent.
Alright.

00:14:58.210 --> 00:14:59.780
Here we go.
Question one.

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:04.472
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received

00:15:04.472 --> 00:15:09.017
as a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would it be?

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:18.875
&gt;&gt; Immediately what comes to minds,
is just a slide that says pause more.

00:15:18.875 --> 00:15:20.480
&gt;&gt; Tell me more about that.

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:23.450
I'm going to take a pause
before you answer though.

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:24.120
Okay, go ahead.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:24.960
Tell me more.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:26.310
&gt;&gt; I love talking about this topic.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:27.620
I love talking about this material.

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:31.458
When I get excited about
something I just get on a roll and

00:15:31.458 --> 00:15:35.963
often string things together that
deserve more breathing room.

00:15:35.963 --> 00:15:40.399
And as a communicator, I am working on

00:15:40.399 --> 00:15:45.540
trying to slow down and pause more, right?

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:48.852
silence is such a powerful
tool in multiple dimensions,

00:15:48.852 --> 00:15:53.130
whether it's giving someone else
the chance for their brain to catch up,

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:57.408
whether it's for you to slow down and
then be able to observe new things and

00:15:57.408 --> 00:16:01.060
take in detail in a way that
you weren't previously.

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.343
So I think that my excitement and

00:16:03.343 --> 00:16:09.020
enthusiasm sometimes just revs up the
speed and the pace at which I'm talking.

00:16:09.020 --> 00:16:15.401
And really, just slowing down and
pausing occasionally can be so helpful.

00:16:15.401 --> 00:16:18.066
&gt;&gt; Not only does it help you think and
formulate, but

00:16:18.066 --> 00:16:19.980
it also helps the audience as well.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:24.381
And I am in your camp,
when I get excited, I talk more than less.

00:16:24.381 --> 00:16:29.796
Let me ask question number two, who is
a communicator that you admire and why?

00:16:32.372 --> 00:16:36.949
&gt;&gt; The first person who jumps to
mind right now is Phoebe Robinson,

00:16:36.949 --> 00:16:39.167
who is a comedian and a TV host.

00:16:39.167 --> 00:16:43.373
And she's now running a book imprint,
publishing imprint.

00:16:43.373 --> 00:16:48.268
I feel like she just has this incredible
facility with language, and she's

00:16:48.268 --> 00:16:53.870
constantly inventing words, and yet you
completely understand what she's saying.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:55.300
And somehow it's like so

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:59.805
much funnier because it's in this sort
of like language of her own invention.

00:16:59.805 --> 00:17:06.611
And it's really body and satirical and
it's just just very, very funny.

00:17:06.611 --> 00:17:11.465
And I suspect that she is making so
many specific decisions

00:17:11.465 --> 00:17:15.634
about her language choice and
her created words.

00:17:15.634 --> 00:17:18.309
And I'd love to just get
a peek inside her brain and

00:17:18.309 --> 00:17:21.441
understand more about how she
thinks about these things.

00:17:21.441 --> 00:17:24.390
&gt;&gt; What a true compliment,
when you want to really understand

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:28.009
somebody's thought process that
leads them to their communication.

00:17:28.009 --> 00:17:29.653
Let me ask the final question.

00:17:29.653 --> 00:17:36.491
What are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:17:36.491 --> 00:17:42.502
&gt;&gt; Maybe the most important
ingredient is caring about the topic.

00:17:42.502 --> 00:17:46.561
I think the second one for me is about

00:17:46.561 --> 00:17:51.851
distilling the content
into its barest form.

00:17:51.851 --> 00:17:56.808
And then for me, I'm often communicating
in a visual medium, as are so many people.

00:17:56.808 --> 00:18:01.231
So I think I immediately just leap to
thinking about designing a keynote or

00:18:01.231 --> 00:18:03.025
a PowerPoint presentation.

00:18:03.025 --> 00:18:07.933
Which so many people have,
as a human race, we have wasted so

00:18:07.933 --> 00:18:12.666
much time trying to read size
eight fonts on [LAUGH] a slide.

00:18:12.666 --> 00:18:13.903
It's just so sad.

00:18:13.903 --> 00:18:19.889
So when I'm thinking about using visuals
to amplify the presentation I'm giving or

00:18:19.889 --> 00:18:24.947
the ideas I'm trying to share,
I think about what I'm excited about,

00:18:24.947 --> 00:18:28.001
my passion or commitment to the material.

00:18:28.001 --> 00:18:31.982
I mean, and this is true even if I'm
giving a budget report in the dean's

00:18:31.982 --> 00:18:34.681
office, I want to see some
student faces on that.

00:18:34.681 --> 00:18:39.945
I want to always be reminded of the
purpose behind the work that we're doing.

00:18:39.945 --> 00:18:44.043
And then think about the simplest
form that I can use,

00:18:44.043 --> 00:18:48.609
and the fewest words, and
how much of it can become visual.

00:18:48.609 --> 00:18:49.770
So let me go back and

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:53.405
try to boil that down into three
[LAUGH] simple ingredients.

00:18:53.405 --> 00:18:58.490
I think it's about interest and
passion for the content,

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:03.587
simplicity, and
bringing things to life in a visual way.

00:19:03.587 --> 00:19:07.131
&gt;&gt; I think those are three
very powerful ingredients and

00:19:07.131 --> 00:19:11.470
can be very helpful for
all the types of communication that we do.

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:13.986
Well, Sarah, I have to thank you so much.

00:19:13.986 --> 00:19:19.618
Your ideas are super practical,
super helpful, and really, really fun.

00:19:19.618 --> 00:19:21.098
I've enjoyed our conversation.

00:19:21.098 --> 00:19:25.358
I appreciate your insights into design,
creativity, and communication.

00:19:25.358 --> 00:19:30.874
And I encourage everyone to get Sarah's
book, Creative Acts for Curious People.

00:19:30.874 --> 00:19:33.343
And then you have to
immediately read it and

00:19:33.343 --> 00:19:36.647
apply these incredibly useful lessons and
activities.

00:19:36.647 --> 00:19:39.749
Thank you for joining us and
thank you for sharing.

00:19:39.749 --> 00:19:40.357
&gt;&gt; My pleasure.

00:19:40.357 --> 00:19:42.090
Thank you so much for having me, Matt.

